 "question": "What does ProcessGpsData() do?",
 "answer": [
  "ProcessGpsData() (nav/navigation.c:45) \u2014 Entry point for every decoded GPS fix (registered as the GPS receiver callback in main).",
  "Step by step, it calls gps_validate_fix() and stores the result in `status` (line 47); then it checks `status != GPS_OK` (GPS_OK = 0) (line 49); if true it calls nav_handle_invalid_fix() and stops there (returns); then it updates g_nav (consecutive_invalid, latitude, longitude, speed_knots, last_fix_ms, has_fix; lines 53\u201358); then it calls CalculateHeading() and stores the result in `currentHeading` (line 59); then it sets `g_nav.mode = NAV_MODE_TRACKING` (line 60).",
  "It is called by handle_rmc() (through the function pointer g_fix_callback). The full chain from the program's start is: main() \u2192 gps_receiver_poll() \u2192 gps_receiver_process_line() \u2192 handle_rmc() \u2192 ProcessGpsData().",
  "The values it updates are used later by NavigationTick() (uses currentHeading, nav_state_t.has_fix, nav_state_t.last_fix_ms, nav_state_t.latitude); nav_handle_invalid_fix() (uses nav_state_t.consecutive_invalid); CalculateHeading() (uses currentHeading)."
 ],
